  "summary": "Traditional Japanese emblems or symbols used by families and institutions to identify lineage and status.",
  "description": "Mon features simplified, symmetrical designs of natural elements, geometric patterns, or abstract representations, rendered in monochrome or limited colors. Originating among the aristocracy and later adopted by all classes, these crests served as visual markers of identity and affiliation in Japanese society.",
  "culturalContext": "The word \"crest\" derives from the Latin crista, meaning \"tuft\" or \"plume\", perhaps related to crinis, \"hair\". Crests had existed in various forms since ancient times: Roman officers wore fans of feathers or horsehair, which were placed longitudinally or transversely depending on the wearer's rank, and Viking helmets were often adorned with wings and animal heads. They first appeared in a heraldic context in the form of the metal fans worn by knights in the 12th and 13th centuries. These were primarily decorative, but may also have served a practical purpose by lessening or deflecting the blows of opponents' weapons (perhaps why their edges came to be serrated). These fans were generally of one colour, later evolving to repeat all or part of the arms displayed on the shield.",
